WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.600
A pulmonary embolism is a
blood clot in the veins of the lung.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.400
It is one of the most dangerous
types of venous thromboembolism,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.600
which is the general term for
when blood clots form in veins.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.100
Pulmonary embolism occurs when
a small blood clot or embolus breaks off

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.480
from a larger clot or thrombus
and travels to the lung,

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.520
where it can cause a potentially
life-threatening blockage.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:31.860
Symptoms include chest pain, shortness of breath,
heart palpitations, coughing up blood,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.760
fainting, and in severe cases, signs
of shock and low blood pressure.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.500
These symptoms are caused
by damage to the lungs,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.940
which also affects the heart —
the right ventricle in particular.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.720
The right side of the heart pumps blood to the
lungs, and is thinner and weaker than the left.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.120
When a pulmonary embolism
blocks the blood vessels of the lung,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.860
there's a sudden increase in
pressure in the pulmonary artery.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.560
This increases the afterload
of the right ventricle,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.780
which is the pressure the heart
must work against to pump blood.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.580
As a result, in some patients, the
right ventricle has to work harder

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:15.040
to keep pumping blood, but
after a certain threshold it fails.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:23.000
This disrupts blood flow to vital organs, leading to death — a process known as haemodynamic collapse.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.720
Individuals at increased risk of
pulmonary embolism include older people,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.520
those with pneumonia, those
undergoing surgery and long hospital stays,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:39.180
people who have major bone fractures with trauma
and people who are immobilised for long periods.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.980
Other medical conditions can also be a risk factor,
such as genetic diseases of the coagulation cascade,

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:50.160
sickle cell disease, and chronic
obstructive pulmonary disease.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.700
There are a number of treatments
available for pulmonary embolism,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:00.020
including antithrombotic treatment with
drugs such as direct oral anticoagulants

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.460
vitamin K antagonists, and low molecular weight heparin.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.320
There are also medical procedures
such as reperfusion therapy

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.680
to restore blood flow to the blocked vessels.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.440
Despite these treatments, up to one in ten
patients will die of venous thromboembolism

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.220
within one year, and at least one in five patients
will have recurrence within five years

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:29.300
if their venous thromboembolism developed without
a clear cause — so-called idiopathic thromboembolism.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.320
Patients who survive can develop post pulmonary embolism syndrome,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.860
a poorly understood condition
characterised by exercise intolerance,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.720
shortness of breath and
a reduction in quality of life.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.640
As research continues, a better
understanding of pulmonary embolism

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.500
and improved treatment should
lead to better chances of survival

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.300
and a better quality of life
for those with this condition.

